  "title": "Node.js memory leak: find it in 10 min",
  "content": "The gap between the demo and the incident report is where this actually lives. After enough code that touches finops patterns gets reviewed, the same failure pattern keeps showing up. Here's what changes once the guessing is replaced with measurement.\n\n## The symptom that never shows up in staging\n\nYou deploy a Node.js service. It runs fine for hours, maybe days. Then RSS climbs, the container gets OOM-killed, and the process restarts. Staging never reproduces it because staging doesn't carry the same traffic mix or stay up long enough. The heap snapshot you take looks unremarkable — until you compare two snapshots taken an hour apart and see a constructor count that only goes up. The real problem is that a leak in a long-running service is a *rate*, not a state, and most teams look for it with the wrong instrument. This post is about matching the instrument to the failure mode before you restart the pod.\n\n## First, read the symptom correctly\n\nNot every rising memory graph is a leak. Before you open a heap profiler, classify what you're seeing. The three patterns below point to different causes and different fixes.\n\n**Pattern A — sawtooth with a rising floor.** RSS climbs, GC brings it down partially, but the trough after each collection is higher than the last. This is the classic leak signature: something is being retained that should be collectable. The retained set grows monotonically.\n\n**Pattern B — flat then cliff.** Memory is stable for hours, then jumps sharply and stays. This is usually a cache or buffer that crossed a size threshold, or a connection pool that grew in response to a burst. It may not be a leak at all — it may be an unbounded data structure that only reveals itself under a specific input.\n\n**Pattern C — high but stable.** RSS sits at a level you don't like but doesn't grow. This is often V8 heap fragmentation or an external buffer (Buffer, ArrayBuffer, native addon) that the GC can't see well. It's a tuning problem, not a leak.\n\nIf you're seeing Pattern A, keep reading. If you're seeing B or C, the heap snapshot workflow below will still help, but the cause is more likely a data structure with no cap than a forgotten listener.\n\nA useful first check that costs nothing: log `process.memoryUsage()` on an interval (every 60 seconds is fine) and watch `heapUsed` versus `rss`. If `heapUsed` is flat but `rss` climbs, the leak is outside the JS heap — native memory, Buffers, or a native addon. If `heapUsed` climbs, you're looking at retained JS objects and the heap snapshot is the right tool.\n\n```javascript\nsetInterval(() => {\n  const m = process.memoryUsage();\n  console.log(JSON.stringify({\n    rss: Math.round(m.rss / 1024 / 1024),\n    heapUsed: Math.round(m.heapUsed / 1024 / 1024),\n    heapTotal: Math.round(m.heapTotal / 1024 / 1024),\n    external: Math.round(m.external / 1024 / 1024),\n  }));\n}, 60_000).unref();\n```\n\nRun this for an hour under real traffic. The shape of those four numbers tells you which of the three patterns you have before you spend time on a snapshot.\n\n## The causes, in the order they actually occur\n\n### 1. Event listeners and subscriptions that outlive their owner\n\nThe most common leak in Node services is an `EventEmitter` listener added per request or per connection and never removed. Express middleware, WebSocket handlers, and message-queue consumers are the usual suspects. The symptom is a listener count that grows with request volume. You can confirm it without a profiler:\n\n```javascript\n// on the emitter in question, during an incident\nconsole.log(emitter.listenerCount('data'));\n```\n\nIf that number tracks your request count, you've found it. Node also emits a `MaxListenersExceededWarning` when a single emitter exceeds its limit (default 10), which is a strong hint even if the warning is buried in logs.\n\n**Fix:** remove the listener in the same scope that added it, ideally in a `finally` block. For request-scoped work, prefer `once` over `on` when the handler should fire a single time. For long-lived subscriptions, tie the listener to an `AbortController` and remove it on abort.\n\n### 2. Closures captured by timers and intervals\n\nA `setInterval` that closes over a request object keeps that object alive for as long as the interval runs. If the interval is never cleared, every request that touched it is retained. This is subtle because the interval itself is small — it's the captured scope that's large.\n\n**Confirm:** search for `setInterval` and check whether each one has a matching `clearInterval` on a shutdown path. In a heap snapshot, look for `Timeout` objects whose retained size is dominated by a closure scope you recognize.\n\n**Fix:** clear intervals on shutdown and on the event that makes them irrelevant. Use `timer.unref()` if the timer should not keep the process alive, but note that `unref()` does not clear the interval — it only changes whether it holds the event loop open.\n\n### 3. Unbounded caches and maps\n\nAn in-memory cache is a leak with good intentions. A `Map` used as a cache grows until something evicts from it, and if nothing does, it grows until the process dies. The symptom is Pattern B: flat, then a cliff as the map crosses a threshold.\n\n**Confirm:** log `map.size` on an interval. If it only goes up, you have your answer.\n\n**Fix:** use an LRU with a maximum size, or a TTL cache with active expiry. If you're on a small budget and don't want a dependency, a `Map` plus a periodic sweep that deletes entries older than a timestamp is enough — but write the sweep, don't assume it exists.\n\n### 4. Promises and async contexts that never settle\n\nA promise that never resolves retains its closure and everything reachable from it. This shows up as a slow leak in services that do fan-out requests with no timeout. The fix is to put a timeout on every outbound call and to make sure the timeout path actually rejects rather than silently dropping.\n\n**Confirm:** in a heap snapshot, look for a large number of `Promise` objects with retained sizes pointing at request payloads. If the count grows with uptime, this is likely.\n\n**Fix:** wrap outbound calls with `AbortSignal.timeout(ms)` where the client supports it, and ensure the rejection is handled. An unhandled rejection that's swallowed by an empty `.catch(() => {})` is worse than a crash — it hides the leak.\n\n### 5. Native memory and Buffers\n\nBuffers allocated outside the V8 heap don't show up in `heapUsed`. If `rss` climbs while `heapUsed` is flat, look at `external` and `arrayBuffers` in `process.memoryUsage()`. Common causes are pooled Buffers that are never released, native addons with their own allocators, and stream pipelines where the readable side is paused and the writable side keeps buffering.\n\n**Confirm:** compare `external` growth to `rss` growth. If they track each other, the leak is in Buffer or native territory.\n\n**Fix:** for streams, respect backpressure — don't `pipe` into a slow consumer without checking `write()` return values, and don't buffer an entire response in memory when you can stream it.\n\n## Taking a heap snapshot that's actually useful\n\nA single snapshot tells you what's in memory. Two snapshots tell you what's *accumulating*. The difference is where the leak lives.\n\nTake the first snapshot after the service has warmed up (say, 10 minutes after deploy), then take a second one an hour later under similar load. In Chrome DevTools, load both and use the \"Comparison\" view. Sort by \"# Delta\" — the constructors with the largest positive delta are your candidates. Then expand the retainer paths for the top few and look for the frame in *your* code, not in a library.\n\nOn a server without a GUI, you can write snapshots to disk and pull them locally:\n\n```javascript\nconst v8 = require('node:v8');\nconst fs = require('node:fs');\n\nfunction snapshot(label) {\n  const file = `/tmp/heap-${label}-${Date.now()}.heapsnapshot`;\n  v8.writeHeapSnapshot(file);\n  console.log('wrote', file);\n}\n\n// call snapshot('warm') once, snapshot('later') an hour later\n```\n\nTwo practical warnings. First, `writeHeapSnapshot` blocks the event loop for the duration of the write — on a large heap this can be seconds. Do it on a canary instance, not on the whole fleet. Second, the snapshot file is roughly the size of the heap, so make sure `/tmp` has room and that you delete old files.\n\nThe retainer path is the important part. It answers \"why is this object still alive?\" — and the answer is usually a global, a module-level variable, or a closure held by a long-lived object. If the retainer path runs through a library you don't control, the fix is usually to stop giving that library new objects, not to patch the library.\n\n## When the heap looks fine but the container still dies\n\nIf `heapUsed` is stable and `rss` still climbs, you're outside V8. Three things to check, in order:\n\n1. **Buffers and streams.** Log `external` and `arrayBuffers`. A growing `arrayBuffers` with a flat heap points at stream backpressure or a Buffer pool that isn't being released.\n2. **Native addons.** If you use a database driver or image library with a native component, its allocations are invisible to V8. Check the addon's issue tracker for known leaks and pin to a version that doesn't have them.\n3. **V8 heap fragmentation.** If `heapUsed` is well below `heapTotal` but `rss` is high, the heap has fragmented. This is a tuning problem: adjusting `--max-old-space-size` or the semi-space size can help, but the real fix is usually to reduce allocation churn in hot paths.\n\nA container memory limit that's close to the heap limit is a common configuration mistake. If the container limit is 512 MB and `--max-old-space-size` is 400, you've left 112 MB for the stack, native memory, and Buffers — which may not be enough. Leave headroom, and set the container limit above the sum of what you expect the process to use at peak.\n\n## Prevention: make leaks visible before they page you\n\nYou can't fix a leak you can't see. Three practices that cost little and catch most of the problems above:\n\n**Log memory on a schedule and alert on slope, not level.** A service at 400 MB that's been flat for a week is fine. One at 200 MB that's climbed 20 MB a day is not. Alert on the derivative.\n\n**Cap every in-memory structure.** If you write `new Map()` in a service, the next line should be a comment explaining what bounds it. If there's no bound, it's a leak waiting for traffic.\n\n**Test with a soak, not a spike.** Load tests that run for five minutes won't reveal a leak that needs an hour. A nightly soak against a staging environment with production-like traffic is the cheapest leak detector you can build.\n\nA minimal soak check you can add to CI:\n\n```javascript\n// after a 30-minute soak against staging, assert heap growth is bounded\nconst before = await getHeapUsed();\nawait runSoak({ durationMs: 30 * 60 * 1000 });\nconst after = await getHeapUsed();\nconst growthMb = (after - before) / 1024 / 1024;\nif (growthMb > 50) {\n  throw new Error(`heap grew ${growthMb.toFixed(1)} MB during soak`);\n}\n```\n\nThe threshold is illustrative — pick one based on your service's normal allocation pattern, not on a number from a blog post.\n\n## Which tool for which budget\n\nHeap snapshots and `process.memoryUsage()` are free and built in. Beyond that, the choice depends on what you're running and what you're paying for.\n\n| Situation | Reasonable choice | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Single Node process, bootstrapped | `node --inspect` + Chrome DevTools, `v8.writeHeapSnapshot` | No cost, no agent, works on a $5 droplet |\n| Container fleet on a cloud provider | Provider's managed profiling (e.g. Cloud Profiler on GCP, Container Insights on AWS) | Useful when you can't SSH into every pod |\n| Multi-language backend | An APM with continuous profiling (e.g. Datadog, Grafana Pyroscope, New Relic) | The value is correlation with traces, not the profiler alone |\n| Series B with an enterprise agreement | Whatever's already in the contract | Adding a new vendor for memory profiling alone is rarely worth the procurement cycle |\n\nThe trap is buying an APM to solve a leak you haven't classified. A continuous profiler shows you *where* CPU and allocation go; it does not tell you what's retained. For retention, you need a heap snapshot. Use the paid tool to find the hot path, then take a snapshot to find the retainer.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Why does my Node.js memory keep growing but heapUsed is stable?**\n\nBecause the growth is outside the V8 heap. `rss` includes native memory, Buffers, and the code itself. Check `external` and `arrayBuffers` in `process.memoryUsage()` — if they're growing, the leak is in Buffer or native territory, and a JS heap snapshot won't show it. The fix is usually in stream handling or a native addon, not in your application objects.\n\n**How many heap snapshots do I need to find a leak?**\n\nTwo, taken under similar load at different times. A single snapshot shows what's in memory; the comparison between two shows what's accumulating. Take the first after warm-up and the second an hour or more later, then sort by delta in DevTools. If the delta is small, you may need a longer interval or heavier traffic to make the leak visible.\n\n**What causes MaxListenersExceededWarning in production?**\n\nA single `EventEmitter` has more than its configured limit of listeners (default 10). In a service, this almost always means a listener is being added per request or per connection without a matching removal. Check `emitter.listenerCount(eventName)` — if it tracks request volume, you've found the leak. The fix is to remove the listener in the same scope that added it.\n\n**When should I restart the pod instead of debugging?**\n\nWhen the service is degraded and the leak is not yet understood, restart to restore service — but capture `process.memoryUsage()` and, if possible, a heap snapshot before the restart. A restart without a snapshot destroys the evidence. If you're restarting on a schedule to mask a leak, that's a signal to schedule the debugging, not a fix.\n\n## The next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your service's main entry file and add the `process.memoryUsage()` interval logger shown above, then deploy it to one canary instance and let it run for an hour. When you come back, compare `heapUsed` and `rss` — that single comparison tells you whether you're hunting a JS retention leak or a native-memory leak, and which of the sections above to read first.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [Google SRE Book: Monitoring Distributed Systems](https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
